#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065430 :tutorial/content "There are two ways to add a positive number to a negative number. Let's work out the value of $-5+7$ using both methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Picturing a Number Line**\n\nWhen we add, we move to the right on the number line.\n\nSo, to add $7$ to $-5$, we start at $-5$ on a number line and move $7$ spaces to the right.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f99b09208d09824dc7c4e6dfaafdc0e20feb7fde00f821a57a70d79196936d.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-5+7 =2\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Subtraction in Reverse**\n\nAdding a positive number to a negative number is like *subtraction in reverse*. To turn the addition into a subtraction, we must switch the numbers around.\n\nSo, to work out ${\\color{red}{-5}} + {\\color{blue}{7}}$, we can switch the numbers around to get\n\n$$\n\\,{\\color{blue}{7}} {\\color{red}{\\,-\\,5}} =2\n$$\n\nWe're allowed to swap the numbers because addition is **commutative**, which means the order in which we add two numbers does not matter." :tutorial/id #uuid "fea61476-1945-5134-8063-3e50b2865f8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8382 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065431 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 5 3\n$$\n\nSince both fractions have a common denominator of $3$, we can combine them to form one fraction:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{3} + \\dfrac{5}{3} =\\dfrac{-1+5}{3}\n$$\n\nTo compute the numerator, we can use either method that we've learned.\n\n**Method 1 - Picturing a Number Line**\n\nTo add $5$ to $-1$, we start at $-1$ on a number line and move $5$ spaces to the right.\n\n![](images/63/632e4bcce5b619f9a5c4dfb2c414a33da0e9567b7b4d8b15adb439da1b3dd88d.png)\n\nWe end up at $4$. So\n\n$$\n-1+5=4\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-1+5}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Subtraction in Reverse**\n\nWe can swap the order of the numbers in the numerator and then compute the resulting subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-1 + 5}{3} = \\frac{5 - 1}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac1 3 + \\dfrac 5 3 = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator *first* and *then* subtract!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9522d6a9-a066-57d7-bf8a-e4baf0467548" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8383 :tutorial/title "Adding a Positive Fraction to a Negative Fraction"}]}
